   "verdict": "DIRECTION IS MODEL-DEPENDENT, reported without smoothing. SINGLE-COPY READOUT ALONE (Sec 5.2's actual claim) moves n* consistently SMALLER -- it collapses to n*=2 in 11/12 cells (shifts -3..-7) -- because uncorrected readout gives the single-copy estimator a large budget-INDEPENDENT bias floor (~-0.5 on a purity of ~0.81; 14.2d bias is unchanged from M=2000 to M=8000 while variance shrinks). SYMMETRIC readout on BOTH sides moves n* consistently LARGER or unchanged, because the collective route measures 2n qubits to the single-copy's n and so bears more readout under any per-qubit model. That symmetric direction is dominated by the collective parity contraction (1-2f)^(2n), a HARSH UPPER BOUND: the destructive-SWAP estimator is an AND-parity prod (-1)^(a_q AND b_q), whose true readout contraction is milder, so a corrected collective model would move the symmetric direction back toward the single-only 'smaller n'. CONCLUSION: the single-copy-side effect robustly confirms and sharpens Sec 5.2 (readout on the single-copy side -> smaller n, strongly); but the crossover does NOT uniformly move smaller once the collective side also bears readout, so Sec 5.2's hedge should be sharpened for the single-copy-side statement, not replaced with an unconditional one. Model caveats: 14.4."
